Part 1:  (The Frontend - React)

The frontend is built inside the frontend/ directory using React + Vite. It manages user authentication, tabs, modals, and the live status of the analysis pipeline.


1. Main Application Dashboard

File: frontend/src/App.jsx

Key Functions:
fetchStatus(): Polls /api/status every 1.5 seconds when an analysis is running to update the loading steps.
handleUpload(): Uploads a local audio file via /api/upload.
handleAnalyze(): Gathers keys, cookies, and source details, and triggers the /api/analyze POST request.
handleChat(): Sends user questions to /api/chat to speak with the RAG assistant.
handleClearChat(): Clears message history.


2. User 

File: frontend/src/LoginPage.jsx

Key Functions:
doLogin(): Submits credentials to /api/auth/login and saves the user token.
handleRegisterSubmit(): Registers new users via /api/auth/register.


3. Task Assigning Popup

File: frontend/src/TaskAssignModal.jsx

Key Functions:
parseActionItems(): Regular expressions split the raw action items text into objects with titles and assignee hints.
resolveAssignee(): Matches text names (e.g. "Rahul") with active user accounts.
handleAssign(): Sends parallel REST calls to create the tasks on the backend.

Detailed Function Explanation:
- mergeUsers(fetchedUsers): Combines a default list of team members (like Ayush, Maria, Rahul) with the list of users downloaded from the database server.
- normalizeName(value): Cleans up text names by making them lower-case, stripping extra spaces, and trimming edges.
- resolveAssignee(assigneeHint, users): Looks at a person's name extracted from the meeting transcript (e.g. "Rahul") and automatically matches it to their database profile username ("@rahul").
- parseActionItems(raw): Scans the raw text summary from the AI and pulls out numbered tasks, deadlines, and suggested owners (e.g. Person: Maria).
- TaskRow(...): The visual template card for a single task row showing the task input, assignee dropdown, priority selector, and due date calendar.
- TaskAssignModal(...): The main popup window component that orchestrates opening action items, loading users, and handling the submit requests.



4. Project Kanban Board

File: frontend/src/TaskManagementPage.jsx

Key Functions:
loadData(): Fetches the overall list of users and active tasks.
handleReassign(): Transfers task ownership.
handleUpdateStatus(): Drags/drops or switches status between Pending, In Progress, and Done.
handleDelete(): Deletes tasks (admin only).


--------------------------------------------------


Part 2:  (The Backend Server - FastAPI)

The server acts as the central router, verifying tokens and modifying the SQLite database.


File: server.py

Key Endpoints & Functions:
@app.post("/api/auth/register"): Validates and inserts new credentials using bcrypt password hashing.
@app.post("/api/auth/login"): Validates credentials and generates bearer authentication tokens.
@app.post("/api/upload"): Receives multi-part file uploads and writes them to the downloads/ directory.
@app.post("/api/analyze"): Kicks off the background worker thread.
@app.get("/api/tasks"): Queries the SQLite database to return tasks. Admins see all tasks, members only see their own.
@app.post("/api/tasks"): Checks permissions and creates a task entry.
@app.patch("/api/tasks/{task_id}"): Modifies task properties.
@app.delete("/api/tasks/{task_id}"): Deletes a task.


--------------------------------------------------


Part 3:  (The Core AI Engine)

These backend modules process audio and run prompts using LangChain + Mistral AI.


1. The Transcriber

File: core/transcriber.py

Key Functions:
transcribe_all(): Master loop processing chunks sequentially.
transcribe_chunk(): Routes chunks to either local OpenAI Whisper or the Sarvam AI API (for Hinglish translations).


2. The Summarizer

File: core/summarizer.py

Key Functions:
split_transcript(): Uses RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter to cut text into 3,000-character segments.
summarize(): Uses a map-reduce pipeline. First, it summarizes segments (map_prompt), then combines them into one final summary (combined_prompt).
generate_title(): Instructs Mistral to output a professional title under 8 words.


3. The Extractor

File: core/extractor.py

Key Functions:
extract_action_items(): Prompts Mistral to extract who does what and deadlines.
extract_key_decisions(): Extracts critical decisions.
extract_questions(): Extracts unresolved questions.


4. Vector Storage

File: core/vector_store.py

Key Functions:
build_vector_store(): Splits transcription into small chunks (500 chars), embeds them with HuggingFace (all-MiniLM-L6-v2), and inserts them into a local Chroma DB store.
get_retriever(): Converts Chroma into a similarity retriever object.


5. RAG Chain

File: core/rag_engine.py

Key Functions:
build_rag_chain(): Configures the QA pipeline.
ask_question(): Feeds your question and matched context to Mistral, returning the final response.


--------------------------------------------------


Part 4: (The Audio Processor)

File: utils/audio_processor.py

Key Functions:
process_input(): The orchestrator.
download_youtube_audio(): Invokes yt-dlp to fetch sound tracks from YouTube URLs.
convert_to_wav(): Translates MP3/MP4 files to clean WAV format.
chunk_audio(): Splits long audio files using pydub into smaller segments so they don't exceed AI limits.



1. Which Frontend Function is Called?
When you click the orange Assign 10 Tasks button:

File: 

frontend/src/TaskAssignModal.jsx
Function: handleAssign()
This function collects all tasks that have a user selected in the "Assign to" dropdown and sends them to the backend server by calling: requestJson('/api/tasks', token, { method: 'POST', body: ... })

2. Which Backend Function is Called?
The server receives the network request at the /api/tasks endpoint:

File: 

server.py
Function: create_task()
This function checks the user's role permissions (admins can assign tasks to anyone; members can only assign tasks to themselves) and runs the query to save them.

3. Where is the Data Stored?
The tasks are saved inside a database file in your project folder:

File Path: c:\Users\ayush\OneDrive\Desktop\transcriva ai summarizer\tasks.db
Database System: SQLite
Database Table: tasks
The backend runs this SQL query to write the data permanently:

sql
INSERT INTO tasks (
    id, title, description, assigned_to, assigned_by, 
    meeting_title, due_date, priority, status, created_at, updated_at
) 
VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?);




When you assign a task—either via the Assign Meeting Tasks modal or manually—here is exactly what happens behind the scenes:

1. Frontend Pre-Resolution & Setup
Extracting Tasks: The 

TaskAssignModal
 parses the action items from the meeting transcription.
Loading Users: It fetches the list of available team members from the backend (/api/users).
Auto-Matching: The 

resolveAssignee
 helper attempts to auto-match names found in action items to actual usernames (e.g., mapping "ayush" or "maria").
2. Clicking "Assign Tasks" (Frontend API Calls)
When you click Assign Tasks, the 

handleAssign
 function:

Filters out any unassigned action items.
Sends parallel POST requests to /api/tasks for each assigned task, passing the task payload (title, description, assignee, creator, due date, priority, and status) along with the authorization token.
3. Backend Validation & Database Persistence (FastAPI)
The requests hit the 

create_task
 endpoint in the backend, performing these checks and operations:

Authorization Check: Verifies who is assigning the task. Non-admin users are only allowed to assign tasks to themselves; assigning to others returns a 403 Forbidden error.
Assignee Verification: Checks the database to ensure the assignee exists in the system.
Data Generation: Generates a unique task ID (e.g., task_4d5b2...) and records timestamps (created_at and updated_at).
Database Write: Inserts the structured task into the SQLite database (tasks table) inside tasks.db.
Response: Commits the changes and returns the newly created task object to the frontend.
4. UI Update
The modal transitions to a success screen showing how many tasks were assigned.
These tasks now dynamically populate the Task Management Board for admins or under My Tasks for individual team members via /api/tasks fetches.






What is a Kanban Board?
A Kanban Board is a visual way to track tasks and see how work is moving from start to finish.

Imagine you have a whiteboard divided into 3 vertical columns:

Pending (To Do): Tasks that are waiting to be started (e.g., "Design overall project architecture").
In Progress: Tasks that team members are working on right now (e.g., "Maria is developing the float meeting interface").
Done: Tasks that are completely finished (e.g., "Uploaded meeting audio").
How does it work in this project?
Every task is represented by a small card on the screen.
When you start working on a task, you drag it or move it from the Pending column to the In Progress column.
Once you finish the task, you move it to the Done column.
This is managed by the function handleUpdateStatus() inside 

TaskManagementPage.jsx
, which instantly saves the new column status to the SQLite database.



